Edition No. 28 — Wednesday, 28 October 2026

10 stories: Thirty ministries and two familiar faces: Parliament approves Hichilema's realignment a day after it was tabled, as Felix Mutati and Douglas Syakalima are nominated and sworn in (lead); 'I am surprised there is no Ministry of Ministers': the cost argument over 30 ministries, and the case made for Electricity, Petroleum, Housing and Culture; Leave PAYE alone, put 15 per cent into health, or do not present it at all: the asks pile up ahead of a 2027 budget scheduled for Friday; A 150 MW coal plant for Ndola and 245 MWp of solar for Mufulira and Kasama: the ERB clears 61 licences and permits worth K13.4 billion in proposed investment; 5,595 confiscated, 27 found: the Mpika North MP calls the Auditor-General's report a crime scene and demands an investigation into the missing Mukula logs; A train held at Mapepe: 25 passengers on the Kitwe–Livingstone service taken to the Chilanga treatment centre, as the national count passes 1,480 cases and 29 deaths; Out after eleven days: the High Court grants Lima MP Kelvin Kaunda K30,000 bail on an assault charge, the Zambian Observer reports, as he denies 'widespread violence' in court; Four wins, 22 goals and a seven-goal afternoon for Maxen Msimuko: Zambia's under-15s finish second in their group at the first FIFA U-15 World Cup and go into Tier B; Chulu and Mbevya win in Monze as MO Drive take a one-two in round three of the national rally championship, with Zain Sidique still leading the standings; 'Ministry on wheels': the President delivers the bus he promised Adventist youth at July's Big Sabbath, and the Lusaka Conference asks its members to keep praying for him.


